<section xml:id="sec-ch02-02-ScalarValuedFunctions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Scalar-Valued Functions</title>

  <p>
    In the section for vector-valued functions, we looked at functions with
    one input variable and multiple output variables (called curves).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-helix">
    <caption>The helix traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = cosine of t, y = sine of t, z = t over 10, for t from 0 to 10, a curve in xyz-space.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-01. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-helix" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-01.png">
          <shortdescription>The helix traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = cosine of t, y = sine of t, z = t over 10, for t from 0 to 10, a curve in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The helix traced by the vector <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos (t), \sin (t), \frac{t}{10}\right\rangle</m>, for <m>t</m> from 0 to 10, a curve in <m>xyz</m>-space.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    In this section, we will look at functions of the form <m>f(x, y)=z</m>,
    having two input variables and one output variable (called surfaces).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-surface-sin">
    <caption>The surface z equals the sine of, x plus y squared, in xyz-space.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-surface-sin" variant="controls" width="90%" aspect="3:2"
      calcplot3d="type=z;z=sin(x+y%5E2);funname=f;visible=true;umin=-4;umax=4;vmin=-4;vmax=4;grid=100;format=normal;alpha=-1;hidemyedges=true;constcol=rgb(255,0,0);view=0;contourcolor=red;fixdomain=false;activetrace=false;contourplot=true;showcontourplot=false;firstvalue=-1;stepsize=0.2;numlevels=11;list=;uselist=false;xnum=46;ynum=46;show2d=false;hidesurface=false;hidelabels=true;showprojections=false;surfacecontours=true;projectioncolor=rgba(255,0,0,1);showxygrid=false;showxygridonbox=false;showconstraint=false&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=-16.066827977112176,5.97346647927092,10.304112624331555,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0.29319095656853217,-0.39797438587643064,0.8692844477918069,1;transparent=false;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=true;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=false;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-4;xmax=4;ymin=-4;ymax=4;zmin=-4;zmax=4;xscale=2;yscale=2;zscale=2;zcmin=-8;zcmax=8;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.35">
      <instructions>
        <p>
          Drag with the mouse to rotate the surface. You can open the plot in a new page from here: <url href="https://c3d.libretexts.org/CalcPlot3D/index.html?type=z;z=sin(x+y%5E2);funname=f;visible=true;umin=-4;umax=4;vmin=-4;vmax=4;grid=100;format=normal;alpha=-1;hidemyedges=true;constcol=rgb(255,0,0);view=0;contourcolor=red;fixdomain=false;activetrace=false;contourplot=true;showcontourplot=false;firstvalue=-1;stepsize=0.2;numlevels=11;list=;uselist=false;xnum=46;ynum=46;show2d=false;hidesurface=false;hidelabels=true;showprojections=false;surfacecontours=true;projectioncolor=rgba(255,0,0,1);showxygrid=false;showxygridonbox=false;showconstraint=false&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=-16.066827977112176,5.97346647927092,10.304112624331555,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0.29319095656853217,-0.39797438587643064,0.8692844477918069,1;transparent=false;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=true;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=false;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-4;xmax=4;ymin=-4;ymax=4;zmin=-4;zmax=4;xscale=2;yscale=2;zscale=2;zcmin=-8;zcmax=8;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.35" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the plot</url>
        </p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-01-1.png">
          <shortdescription>The surface z equals the sine of, x plus y squared, in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The surface <m>z=\sin \left(x+y^2\right)</m>, in <m>xyz</m>-space.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The outputs of vector-valued functions were vectors. The outputs of
    scalar-valued functions are scalars (numbers).
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Scalar-Valued Functions</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A scalar-valued function of the form <m>z=f(x, y)</m> is a function
        whose domain (set of inputs) is a subset of the <m>x y</m>-plane
        ( <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m> ) and whose range (set of outputs) is a subset
        of the real line ( <m>\mathbb{R}</m> ).
      </p>

      <p>
        The graph of <m>f</m> is called a surface and consists of all points
        ( <m>x, y, f(x, y)</m> ) where <m>(x, y)</m> is in the domain of
        <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-point-on-graph">
    <caption>A point (x, y, f(x,y)) on the graph of a scalar-valued function f.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-02-1. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-point-on-graph" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-02-1.png">
          <shortdescription>A point (x, y, f(x,y)) on the graph of a scalar-valued function f.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>A point <m>(x, y, f(x,y))</m> on the graph of a scalar-valued function <m>f</m>.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the graph of <m>f(x, y)=y^{2}-x^{2}</m> shown below.
        Determine whether the points
        <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{4},-\frac{3}{16}\right)</m> and
        <m>\left(-1, \frac{1}{3}, 2\right)</m> lie on the graph of <m>f</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-1">
        <caption>The graph of f of x and y equals y squared minus x squared, as a surface in xyz-space.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-02. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-02.png">
              <shortdescription>The graph of f of x and y equals y squared minus x squared, as a surface in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The graph of <m>f(x, y)=y^{2}-x^{2}</m>, as a surface in <m>xyz</m>-space.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A point <m>(x_0, y_0, z_0)</m> lies on the graph of <m>f</m> exactly
        when <m>z_0 = f(x_0, y_0)</m>. For the first point:
        <md>
          f\left( \frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{4} \right) = \left( \frac{1}{4} \right)^2 - \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^2 = \frac{1}{16}-\frac{4}{16} = -\frac{3}{16}
        </md>
        which matches the third coordinate, so
        <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{4},-\frac{3}{16}\right)</m> lies on
        the graph of <m>f</m>. For the second point:
        <md>
          f\left( -1, \frac{1}{3} \right) = \left( \frac{1}{3} \right)^2 - (-1)^2 = \frac{1}{9}-1 = -\frac{8}{9} \neq 2
        </md>
        so <m>\left(-1, \frac{1}{3}, 2\right)</m> does not lie on the graph
        of <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Given a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> :
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            To find its domain, denoted <m>\operatorname{dom}(f)</m>,
            determine all <m>(x, y)</m> that can be plugged into <m>f</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            To find its range, denoted <m>\operatorname{rang}(f)</m>,
            determine all values of <m>z</m> that have a pair <m>(x, y)</m>
            mapped to it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-domain-range">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 4.1</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-03.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>The domain and range of a scalar-valued function f(x,y), illustrating how inputs map to outputs.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>The domain and range of a scalar-valued function <m>f(x,y)</m>, illustrating how inputs map to outputs.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the domain and range of the following functions:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Plane, <m>f(x, y)= 4x+ y+1</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Paraboloid, <m>f(x, y)=x^{2}+y^{2}</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Upper Half of a Sphere with Radius 1,
              <m>f(x, y)=\sqrt{1-x^2 -y^{2}}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Any pair <m>(x,y)</m> can be plugged into <m>4x+y+1</m>, so
        <m>\operatorname{dom}(f) = \mathbb{R}^2</m>, the entire
        <m>xy</m>-plane. For the range, fixing <m>y=0</m> the outputs
        <m>f(x,0) = 4x+1</m> already take every real value as <m>x</m>
        varies, so <m>\operatorname{rang}(f) = \mathbb{R}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Again any pair can be plugged in, so
        <m>\operatorname{dom}(f) = \mathbb{R}^2</m>. Since
        <m>x^2+y^2 \geq 0</m>, no negative output is possible. The output 0
        is attained at <m>(0,0)</m>, and every <m>z \gt 0</m> is also
        attained, for instance at <m>(\sqrt{z}, 0)</m>. Hence
        <m>\operatorname{rang}(f) = [0, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here we need the expression under the square root to be
        non-negative:
        <md>
          1-x^2-y^2 \geq 0 \Leftrightarrow x^2+y^2 \leq 1
        </md>
        so <m>\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> is the disk of radius 1 centered at
        the origin (including its boundary circle). The square root is
        non-negative, and the outputs run from <m>f=0</m> (attained on the
        boundary circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>) up to <m>f=1</m> (attained at
        <m>(0,0)</m>), so <m>\operatorname{rang}(f) = [0,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-2">
    <caption>The plane <m>f(x, y)= 4x+ y-1</m>, the paraboloid <m>f(x, y)=x^{2}+y^{2}</m> and the upper half of a sphere <m>f(x, y)=\sqrt{1-x^2 -y^{2}}</m> of <xref ref="example-ch02-02-ex-6-2"/>.</caption>

    <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-2a">
        <caption>The plane f of x and y equals 4 x plus y minus 1, in xyz-space.</caption>
