  </p>
  <!--\caption*{From openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.2}-->

  <definition>
    <title>Planes in <m>x y z</m>-space</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A plane in <m>x y z</m>-space is the set of all points that satisfy
        the equation
        <md>
          a x+b y+c z=d
        </md>, where <m>a, b, c</m>, and <m>d</m> are real numbers.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      In order to find an equation for a plane, all we need is:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A point in the plane.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The orientation (tilt) of the plane.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The orientation is given by a vector orthogonal to every other vector
      in the plane. We call this vector a normal vector to the plane.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-normal-vector">
      <caption>A plane in xyz-space with a normal vector n orthogonal to every vector in the plane.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-normal-vector" variant="controls" width="90%" aspect="3:2"
        calcplot3d="type=z;z=x+y+.25;funname=f;visible=true;umin=-8;umax=8;vmin=-8;vmax=8;grid=10;format=constant;alpha=-1;hidemyedges=false;constcol=rgb(30,136,229);view=0;contourcolor=red;fixdomain=false;activetrace=false;contourplot=true;showcontourplot=false;firstvalue=-1;stepsize=0.2;numlevels=11;list=;uselist=false;xnum=46;ynum=46;show2d=false;hidesurface=false;hidelabels=true;showprojections=false;surfacecontours=true;projectioncolor=rgba(255,0,0,1);showxygrid=false;showxygridonbox=false;showconstraint=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C4,%204,8%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(255,193,7);size=5;initialpt=(0,-.250,0);dashed=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C-4,-%204,%204%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(216,27,96);size=5;initialpt=(0,-.250,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=27.192731773906313,-38.14495714040698,10.465064903230056,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=-0.11655892322221247,0.09515025743330567,0.9886154186171963,1;transparent=true;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=false;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=false;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-8;xmax=8;ymin=-8;ymax=8;zmin=-8;zmax=8;xscale=4;yscale=4;zscale=4;zcmin=-16;zcmax=16;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.331">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Drag with the mouse to rotate the surface. You can open the plot in a new page from here: <url href="https://c3d.libretexts.org/CalcPlot3D/index.html?type=z;z=x+y+.25;funname=f;visible=true;umin=-8;umax=8;vmin=-8;vmax=8;grid=10;format=constant;alpha=-1;hidemyedges=false;constcol=rgb(30,136,229);view=0;contourcolor=red;fixdomain=false;activetrace=false;contourplot=true;showcontourplot=false;firstvalue=-1;stepsize=0.2;numlevels=11;list=;uselist=false;xnum=46;ynum=46;show2d=false;hidesurface=false;hidelabels=true;showprojections=false;surfacecontours=true;projectioncolor=rgba(255,0,0,1);showxygrid=false;showxygridonbox=false;showconstraint=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C4,%204,8%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(255,193,7);size=5;initialpt=(0,-.250,0);dashed=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C-4,-%204,%204%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(216,27,96);size=5;initialpt=(0,-.250,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=27.192731773906313,-38.14495714040698,10.465064903230056,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=-0.11655892322221247,0.09515025743330567,0.9886154186171963,1;transparent=true;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=false;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=false;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-8;xmax=8;ymin=-8;ymax=8;zmin=-8;zmax=8;xscale=4;yscale=4;zscale=4;zcmin=-16;zcmax=16;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.331" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the plot</url>
          </p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-05.png">
            <shortdescription>A plane in xyz-space with a normal vector n orthogonal to every vector in the plane.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>A plane in <m>xyz</m>-space with a normal vector <m>\vec{n}</m> orthogonal to every vector in the plane.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Given a point <m>P_{0}\left(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}\right)</m> on a plane
      <m>\pi</m> and a vector <m>\vec{n}=\langle a, b, c\rangle</m> normal
      to <m>\pi</m>, the equation for <m>\pi</m> is given by
      <md>
        a\left(x-x_{0}\right)+b\left(y-y_{0}\right)+c\left(z-z_{0}\right)=0
      </md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      To find a vector normal to a plane <m>\pi</m>, all you need is the
      cross-product of two non-parallel vectors in the plane (since they
      define the plane uniquely).
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find an equation for the plane that passes through the points
        <m>P(-1,-1,-1), Q(-1,-1,1)</m>, and <m>R(1,1,1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        To find an equation for the plane we need a point in the plane (we
        have three) and a normal vector, which we get as the cross product of
        two non-parallel vectors in the plane. Two vectors in the plane are
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{PQ} = \langle -1-(-1), -1-(-1), 1-(-1) \rangle = \langle 0,0,2 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec{PR} = \langle 1-(-1), 1-(-1), 1-(-1) \rangle = \langle 2,2,2 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
        and they are not parallel (no scalar multiple of
        <m>\langle 0,0,2 \rangle</m> can have a nonzero first component).
        Their cross product is normal to the plane:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{n} = \vec{PQ} \times \vec{PR} = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 0 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} 0 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 0(2)-2(2) \right)\vec{i} - \left( 0(2)-2(2) \right)\vec{j} + \left( 0(2)-0(2) \right)\vec{k} = \langle -4,4,0 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
        Using the point <m>P(-1,-1,-1)</m> and the normal vector
        <m>\vec{n} = \langle -4,4,0 \rangle</m>:
        <md>
          -4\left( x-(-1) \right)+4\left( y-(-1) \right)+0\left( z-(-1) \right) = 0 \Rightarrow -4x+4y = 0 \Rightarrow y = x
        </md>
        As a check, all three points <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m>
        satisfy the equation <m>y=x</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Normal Vector to a Plane</title>

    <p>
      Given the plane <m>a x+b y+c z=d</m>, the vector
      <m>\vec{n}=\langle a, b, c\rangle</m> is a normal vector to the plane.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Whether two planes are parallel, orthogonal, or neither, can be
            answered by asking the same question about their normal vectors.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If two planes are not parallel, they always intersect in a line.
            The direction of this line is given by the cross product of the
            normal vectors to the planes.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-intersecting-planes">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.5</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-07.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>Two non-parallel planes intersecting in a line in xyz-space. The angle between the planes is the same as the angle that the normal vectors form.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>Two non-parallel planes intersecting in a line in <m>xyz</m>-space. The angle between the planes is the same as the angle that the normal vectors form.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!--\begin{figure}[H]
      \includegraphics[max width=0.3\textwidth, center]{2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-07}
      \caption*{Point (6, 4.5, 8)}
      \end{figure}-->

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the planes <m>\pi_{1}</m> given by
        <m>3 x+2 y+ z=30</m> and <m>\pi_{2}</m> given by
        <m>-3 x+4 y+6 z=0</m> are parallel, orthogonal or neither.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The normal vectors are <m>\vec{n}_1 = \langle 3,2,1 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{n}_2 = \langle -3,4,6 \rangle</m>, and we answer the question
        by asking it about them. If they were parallel there would be a scalar
        <m>c</m> with <m>c\vec{n}_1 = \vec{n}_2</m>; the first components
        force <m>c=-1</m> while the second components force <m>c=2</m>, so
        the normal vectors (and hence the planes) are not parallel. Checking
        orthogonality with the dot product:
        <md>
          \vec{n}_1 \cdot \vec{n}_2 = 3(-3)+2(4)+1(6) = -9+8+6 = 5 \neq 0
        </md>
        so the normal vectors are not orthogonal either. Hence the planes are
        neither parallel nor orthogonal.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find an equation of the line where the planes <m>\pi_{1}</m> and
        <m>\pi_{2}</m> intersect where
        <md>
          \pi_{1}:3x+2 y+ z=1 ; \quad \pi_{2}: x+y+z=1
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The normal vectors are <m>\vec{n}_1 = \langle 3,2,1 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{n}_2 = \langle 1,1,1 \rangle</m>, which are not scalar
        multiples of each other, so the planes are not parallel and they
        intersect in a line. The direction of this line is given by the cross
        product of the normal vectors:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{v} = \vec{n}_1 \times \vec{n}_2 = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ 3 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} 3 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 2(1)-1(1) \right)\vec{i} - \left( 3(1)-1(1) \right)\vec{j} + \left( 3(1)-2(1) \right)\vec{k} = \langle 1,-2,1 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
        We also need a point on the line, that is, a point satisfying both
        plane equations. Subtracting the equation of <m>\pi_2</m> from the
        equation of <m>\pi_1</m> eliminates <m>z</m>:
        <md>
          (3x+2y+z)-(x+y+z) = 1-1 \Rightarrow 2x+y = 0
        </md>
        Choosing <m>x=0</m> gives <m>y=0</m>, and then the equation of
        <m>\pi_2</m> gives <m>z=1</m>, so the point <m>(0,0,1)</m> lies on
        both planes. Hence the line of intersection is
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle 0,0,1 \rangle + t\langle 1,-2,1 \rangle = \langle t, -2t, 1+t \rangle, \quad -\infty \lt t \lt \infty
        </md>
        As a check, substituting the parametric equations into both plane
        equations: <m>3t-4t+(1+t) = 1</m> and <m>t-2t+(1+t) = 1</m> for
        every <m>t</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    Consider the picture of Devil's Tower and a topographical map for it
    given below:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-devils-tower">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 4.1</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-09-1.jpg" width="70%">
      <shortdescription>A photograph of Devils Tower and its topographical map, showing level curves at constant elevation.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A photograph of Devils Tower and its topographical map, showing level curves at constant elevation.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    To get such a topographical map, you:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Think of Devil's Tower as a surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Intersect that surface with planes of the form <m>z=c</m> (planes
          parallel to the <m>x y</m>-plane).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    When you intersect a surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m> with a plane <m>z=c</m>,
    you get a curve called a level curve, given by the equation
    <m>c=f(x, y)</m>. (<url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/vmgur5hd03" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">graph</url>)
  </p>

  <!--\begin{figure}[H]
      \begin{center}
      \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-09}
      \captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
      \caption{$z=4x^2+4y^2 \text{ intersected with } z=2$}\end{center}
      \end{figure}-->

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-level-curve">
    <caption><m>z=\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{y^2}{4} \text{ intersected with } z=2</m></caption>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-level-curve-a">
        <caption>The paraboloid z equals x squared over 2 plus y squared over 4, intersected with the plane z equals 2.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-09. -->
