  <p>
    You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Describe the level curve you get when intersecting the cone
        <m>z=</m> <m>\sqrt{2x^{2}+2y^{2}}</m> with the plane <m>z=1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-6">
        <caption>The cone z equals the square root of, 2 x squared plus 2 y squared, intersected with the plane z equals 1.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-10. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-6" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-10.png">
              <shortdescription>The cone z equals the square root of, 2 x squared plus 2 y squared, intersected with the plane z equals 1.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The cone <m>z=\sqrt{2x^{2}+2y^{2}}</m>, intersected with the plane <m>z=1</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The level curve is defined by the equation
        <m>1 = \sqrt{2x^2+2y^2}</m>. Squaring both sides:
        <md>
          1 = 2x^2+2y^2 \Rightarrow x^2+y^2 = \frac{1}{2}
        </md>
        This is a circle centered at the origin of radius
        <m>\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, drawn at height
        <m>z=1</m> on the cone, as in the picture above.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Traces of Surfaces</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        When you intersect a surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m> with planes of the
        form <m>x=x_{0}</m>, <m>y=y_{0}</m>, and <m>z=z_{0}</m>, the
        corresponding level curve is called a trace of the surface. Vertical
        trace refers to the intersection with a plane of the form
        <m>x=x_0</m> or <m>y=y_0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Describe the vertical trace you get when intersecting the hyperbolic
        paraboloid <m>z=y^{2}-x^{2}</m> with the plane <m>y=1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-7">
        <caption>The hyperbolic paraboloid z equals y squared minus x squared, intersected with the plane y equals 1, showing the vertical trace.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-11. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-7" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-11.png">
              <shortdescription>The hyperbolic paraboloid z equals y squared minus x squared, intersected with the plane y equals 1, showing the vertical trace.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The hyperbolic paraboloid <m>z=y^{2}-x^{2}</m>, intersected with the plane <m>y=1</m>, showing the vertical trace.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Substituting <m>y=1</m> into the equation of the surface:
        <md>
          z = 1^2-x^2 = 1-x^2
        </md>
        The vertical trace is a downward-opening parabola lying in the plane
        <m>y=1</m>, with vertex at the point <m>(0,1,1)</m> and passing
        through the points <m>(\pm 1, 1, 0)</m>, as the picture above shows.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        From Knowledge Check: Which of the following show level curves of the
