          <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
            <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-8c">
              <caption>Option c displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly greater than 1</caption>

              <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-12-3. -->
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-8c" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <!--<instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>-->

                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-12-3.png">
                    <shortdescription>Option c displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly greater than 1</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Option c displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly greater than 1</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-8d">
              <caption>Option d displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly smaller than 1</caption>

              <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-12-2. -->
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-8d" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <!--<instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>-->

                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-12-2.png">
                    <shortdescription>Option d displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly smaller than 1</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Option d displays parallel straight lines with slope significantly smaller than 1</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>
          </sidebyside>
        </sbsgroup>
      </figure>
      <p>
        You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the top left graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the top right graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the bottom left graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the bottom right graph</url>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Setting <m>z=c</m> for a constant <m>c</m>, the level curves are
        given by
        <md>
          c = x-y \Rightarrow y = x-c
        </md>
        For every value of <m>c</m> this is a straight line with slope
        exactly 1 (and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>-c</m>), so the level curves
        form a family of parallel lines of slope 1. Among the figures above,
        (a) shows hyperbola-like curves, while (c) and (d) show parallel
        lines that rise too fast or too slow for their slope to be equal to
        1. Hence the answer is (b).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Suppose you have a function of the form <m>w=F(x, y, z)</m>, which
      corresponds to an object in 4 dimensions.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can intersect this function with a hyperplane of the form
      <m>w=c</m> to get a 3D-surface called a level surface defined by the
      equation <m>c=F(x, y, z)</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>w=x^{2}+2y^{2}+3z^{2}</m>. Describe the
        level surface you get when intersecting <m>w</m> with the hyperplane
        <m>w=4</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The level surface is defined by the equation
        <md>
          x^2+2y^2+3z^2 = 4 \Rightarrow \frac{x^2}{4}+\frac{y^2}{2}+\frac{z^2}{4/3} = 1
        </md>
        This is an ellipsoid centered at the origin, crossing the
        <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=z=0</m>) at <m>x=\pm 2</m>, the <m>y</m>-axis
        (<m>x=z=0</m>) at <m>y=\pm\sqrt{2}</m>, and the <m>z</m>-axis
        (<m>x=y=0</m>) at <m>z=\pm\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}</m>, as shown below.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-02-ex-6-9">
        <caption>The ellipsoid x squared plus 2 y squared plus 3 z squared equals 4.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-13. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-02-ex-6-9" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_06_f7193d1cc61b1e2c50ebg-13.png">
              <shortdescription>The ellipsoid x squared plus 2 y squared plus 3 z squared equals 4.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The ellipsoid <m>x^2+2y^2+3z^2=4</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Implicit vs. Explicit Surfaces</title>

    <p>
      Consider the plane <m>\pi</m> defined by <m>x+2 y-z=4</m>. Since
      <m>z</m> isn't by itself on one side of the equation, we say that
      <m>z</m> is given implicitly as a function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you solve for <m>z</m> and write <m>\pi</m> as <m>z=x+2 y-4</m>,
      then we say <m>z</m> is given explicitly as a function of <m>x</m> and
      <m>y</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same definition of implicit or explicit applies for functions in
      higher dimensions.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-02-ex-6-10">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of the following equations, determine whether <m>z</m> is
        given implicitly or explicitly as a function of <m>x</m> and
        <m>y</m>. If <m>z</m> is given implicitly, write it explicitly (if it
        isn't possible, explain why).
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>z=\sin(x+y+3)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>4z+e^{\sin x-\cos y}= z+\cos x</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>z^{4}+3y^{2}=\log x</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>z</m> is by itself on one side of the equation, <m>z</m> is
        given explicitly as a function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>z</m> appears on both sides of the equation and is not by
        itself, so <m>z</m> is given implicitly. Solving for <m>z</m>:
        <md>
          4z-z = \cos x - e^{\sin x-\cos y} \Rightarrow 3z = \cos x - e^{\sin x-\cos y} \Rightarrow z = \frac{\cos x - e^{\sin x-\cos y}}{3}
        </md>
        so <m>z</m> can be written explicitly.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Again <m>z</m> is given implicitly. Attempting to solve for
        <m>z</m>:
        <md>
          z^4 = \log x - 3y^2 \Rightarrow z = \pm\sqrt[4]{\log x - 3y^2}
        </md>
        It is not possible to write <m>z</m> explicitly as a function of
        <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>: because of the <m>\pm</m>, every input
        <m>(x,y)</m> with <m>\log x - 3y^2 \gt 0</m> corresponds to two
        different values of <m>z</m>, so <m>z</m> is not uniquely determined
        by <m>(x,y)</m> and is therefore not a function of <m>x</m> and
        <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

